WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.070
The world’s most dangerous path.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.370
The world’s most treacherous, the world’s
most deadly.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.530
This got all those descriptors and more.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:10.769
This is El Caminito Del Rey, near Malaga,

00:00:10.769 --> 00:00:12.160
on the southern coast of Spain.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.039
And, well, once this was the world’s most
dangerous path,

00:00:15.039 --> 00:00:16.780
or certainly something very close to it.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:20.920
It was built between 1901 and 1905 by sailors
and prisoners,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.869
who were either used to working at height,

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:24.630
or didn’t really have much of a choice.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.789
And it was used to get people between two
hydroelectric power stations, and yes,

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:31.430
apparently there were hydroelectric power
stations in 1900s Spain.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.050
It surprised me too.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.829
It was named El Caminito del Rey, the king’s
little pathway,

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:39.239
after it was walked by King Alfonso the 13th.

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:41.560
But it didn’t look like this back then.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.230
It was just some metal, hammered into the
rock at 45°,

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.640
and then some concrete put on top of it.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.010
And maybe there’d be a bit of a handrail
or a safety wire,

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:51.810
but it was certainly not safe by modern standards.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.289
And over time, the path fell into disuse,

00:00:54.289 --> 00:00:55.609
and it fell into disrepair,

00:00:55.609 --> 00:00:58.260
and then just started to fall apart.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.440
But that didn’t stop thrill seekers.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.760
As the internet and the web started to spread
news of it,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.980
this attracted people from all over the world
to come and walk

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.080
this crumbling but apparently-just-about-safe
walkway.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.590
And there are photos, all over the web, of
2005 and 2006,

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:19.840
of people climbing onto safety wires and walking
across very narrow bits of metal

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.680
to try to get to the next bit.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.500
But that’s not what it looks like now.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.670
The trouble was: people kept falling to their
deaths,

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.509
at the rate of one every couple of years.

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:30.549
And eventually the local government decided,
well, no,

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:31.549
this is enough.

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:32.659
We are closing this.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.220
Which of course just made the problem worse,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.390
because now thrill seekers want to do the
most dangerous path in the world…

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:38.799
that had been banned.

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:42.840
So eventually they found a solution: build
a brand-new path,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.119
on top of the ruins of the old one,

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:47.450
and build it to 21st-century standards with
21st-century technology.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.299
There are safety helmets now.

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:51.000
There are handrails, there’s wire,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.830
there is a guard at the entrance checking
tickets.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:56.710
And there is wi-fi and toilets at each end.

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:58.060
And you know what?

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.060
It works.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.619
It’s still thrilling, but it’s also safe,
and now:

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:05.909
there are a heck of a lot more people who
have access to this incredible site and the

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:06.909
views.

00:02:06.909 --> 00:02:07.939
But online?

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:10.380
This is still the most dangerous path in the
world.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.730
You will still see the old blog posts from
the 2000s,

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:18.520
you will still see the old image sharing sites
and YouTube videos from five years ago saying,

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.270
"oh yeah, this is still dangerous.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:21.270
This is still terrifying."

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.440
And of course nobody bothers fact checking
online,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.850
so, oh, this still gets forwarded everywhere.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.420
But this isn’t that dangerous any more.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.390
It used to be as dangerous as, say, a base
jump,

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:31.890
it used to attract thrill seekers,

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.140
the real adrenaline junkies... and now?

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.670
It attracts tourists who are okay with heights.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.040
And I reckon that’s better.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.590
Even if the click bait might not be true any
more,

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.870
a heck of a lot more people have access to
what is an incredible place.

